Dave Burns wrote 7/24/94: <There is doubt about whether Lao Tzu was a single
person, or a mythical author for a collection of verses that have diverse
authors and origins (translation of Lao Tzu = Old Man). His (mythical?)
occupation was wanderer, hermit, etc., but not religious leader. One might as
well call him an early scientist - the distinction between religion, philosophy,
and science would not have meant much to the people of that time and place. He
didn't claim that any supernatural beings accosted him in the form of a burning
bush with instructions for proper behavior.>

True.  Very little hard evidence on this topic.  Linguistic analysis of Tao Te
Ching text indicates very similar style and content but not conclusive whether
it's one author or not.  No exact dates, seems to have filtered into public
awareness over a stretch of time.  Text is overwhelmingly holistic oriented, not
analytical/reductionist.  Difficult to portray as a scientist in the modern
specialized sense of the word (except for cosmologists).  Much more like a
purpose giver, or a poet.  Religious leaders do not have to try to be one, it is
the historical fact of people adopting the ideas religiously that, by default,
make someone into a religious leader.  There are many religious leaders today in
the leftist environmental movement, in the radical feminists, and the leftist
statists who would disavow the label, but their dependents follow them
religiously.

I would also point out that birthdates of several people on the list were
approximates due to lack of hard evidence.  Also, there is mounting evidence
that Zarathustra may have been one or more people originating as early as 2000
bc instead of 600 bc.  The linkages in ideas are to the Proto-Indo-Europeans of
the Central Asian steppes.

Phil Fraering wrote 7/25/94: <I have noticed an extreme paucity of Roman
individuals on your list, Reilly. I was just wondering if you wanted to
comment on that.>

The list is pretty long already.  Here are some more Romans, from the second
tier.  By and large, the early Romans did not originate much in the way of
memetics.  They were analogous to the medieval Scholastics in that they simply
worked up derivatives of the original Greek Extropic thought.  The patricians
were Stoic, the plebeians were Epicurean, they didn't originate any new outlook
besides these.  By the time of Juvenal, empire-wide stagnant defensive mode had
set in, entropic to the core.  Satire appears at the end of the creative spark
of civilization.  This is the short answer.

Cicero           (105)   Orator
Julius Caesar    (100)   Conqueror
Lucretius         (95)   Philosopher
Virgil            (70)   Poet
Livy              (64)   Historian
Augustus Caesar   (63)   Conqueror
