From: mlinksva@netcom.com (Mike Linksvayer)
Date: Tue, 26 Jul 1994 18:27:17 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-7-221] REVIEW: _Interface_ by Stephen Bury

I feel slightly surprised that I have not seen any mention on this list
of _Interface_ by Stephen Bury.

Bury, better known as Neal Stephenson, author of _Snow Crash_, has
written another fun and irreverent near future novel.  _Interface_
takes place during the 1996 U.S. Presidential campaign.  I think Bury
only mentions the date once, almost as if he did not mean to.

In his 1996 state of the union address "the current president"
announces

	"Great sins demand great forgiveness.  Let us now
	forgive ourselves, so that we may go forth into the
	brave new world of the third millennium with a clean
	slate and a clear conscience."

In other words, "The current president" suggests putting a cap on
interest payments on the national debt, now at US$10 trillion.

This move greatly upsets "the Network", a nebulous group of investors
(who happen to use encrypted communications) who stand to lose
trillions over the long term.

The Network reasons that the United States has gone bankrupt, and the
time has come for the bankers to take control of its assets.  To achive
control, the Network tries to work with an independent presidential
candidate from Tuscola, Illinois, who has recently had a stroke, and
has a chip implanted in his brain to replace dead neurons...

_Interface_ does not hold nearly as much interest as _Snow Crash_ does
for extropians.  However, if you liked _Snow Crash_'s attitude, I think
you'll enjoy reading _Interface_ very much.


Mike Linksvayer   mlinksva@netcom.com

